CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a four-column visualization of the activations inside a small neural network, the columns arranged left to right as successive stages of training. Each column is a vertical stack of seven flat square planes in isometric perspective, one plane per layer output, joined by dashed trajectory curves that carry every datapoint from one plane to the next. Points appear in two colors, red and blue. In all four columns the bottom plane shows the two colors thoroughly intermingled in a dense central cluster, and moving upward they are pulled apart, the red trajectories drawing into a tight vertical bundle while the blue ones bow outward to one side. A legend column at the far left gives the architecture as a bottom-to-top chain of arrows and rounded labels reading linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, and softmax. Beneath the panels a long horizontal arrow labeled Training iteration runs the full width, with sets of three dots between columns marking skipped iterations. Reading up a column shows how one forward pass reshapes the data distribution layer by layer; reading across shows the separation growing cleaner and the blue points swinging farther out as training proceeds.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Seven flat square planes stacked vertically with equal spacing, each drawn as an isometric diamond with a dark gray outline and faint interior grid (3x3 subdivision)
  - Bottom plane (input / data layer) with ~60 red and ~60 blue datapoints intermingled in a dense central cluster
  - Plane 2: output of first linear layer
  - Plane 3: output of first relu
  - Plane 4: output of second linear layer
  - Plane 5: output of second relu
  - Plane 6: output of third linear layer
  - Plane 7 (top): output of softmax, red points collapsed to a tight bundle at one corner region, blue points spread to the opposite side
  - Dashed curved trajectory segments connecting every datapoint's position on one plane to its position on the plane above, colored red or blue by class, semi-transparent
  - Small round dot markers at each datapoint's landing position on every plane
  - Faint gray dashed 'hull' lines connecting the outer corner extremes of the point cloud between successive planes (the light gray envelope wireframe)
  - Left legend column: vertical chain of upward arrows alternating with rounded-rectangle labels reading, bottom to top, 'linear', 'relu', 'linear', 'relu', 'linear', 'softmax'
  - Bottom horizontal arrow spanning the figure width labeled 'Training iteration'
  - Ellipsis marks (three dots) along the training-iteration axis indicating skipped iterations
  - Iteration index readout for the currently displayed training stage

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
One column only, centered. Planes are 4 x 4 world-unit squares lying in the horizontal (XZ) plane, stacked along +Y at y = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 (7 planes, spacing 2.0). Plane outline: dark gray #444444, 1.5px, with a faint interior 3x3 grid at #CCCCCC. Isometric pose: squares read as diamonds with their diagonals horizontal/vertical on screen. Legend column sits at the far LEFT of the frame, outside the stack, as a flat 2D overlay: upward arrows alternating with rounded-rect labels, bottom to top 'linear' (between planes 1-2), 'relu' (2-3), 'linear' (3-4), 'relu' (4-5), 'linear' (5-6), 'softmax' (6-7). Training-iteration arrow runs horizontally beneath the whole figure, left to right, with the slider standing in for the four sampled columns. Points: 60 red (#E8000B / pure red #FF0000 at full opacity for foreground dots) and 60 blue (#0000FF), radius ~0.05 world units. Trajectory curves: dashed, 2-3 segment smooth interpolation between consecutive plane positions, opacity ~0.35, same class color; gray envelope lines #AAAAAA dashed at opacity 0.3 connecting extreme points between planes. Point layout per layer, driven by iteration t in [0,3]: bottom plane always a Gaussian cluster of sigma ~0.35 centered at plane origin with red and blue fully interleaved (identical distribution, class assigned by a checkerboard/spiral rule so they are inseparable in raw space). Moving upward, red x/z coordinates contract toward the plane center by factor (1 - 0.25*t*k) per layer while blue coordinates translate outward along +X by up to 1.2*t/3 per layer; at the top softmax plane with t=3, red occupies a ~0.2-radius disc near center-left while blue fans out to 2-6 units along +X, some blue points escaping well beyond the plane boundary (as in the rightmost source column). Relu layers additionally clamp negative coordinates to ~0, visibly flattening part of the cloud against the plane's axes. At t=0 all layers keep red and blue statistically identical and confined within the plane square.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "trainingIteration",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "As optimization proceeds the same input cloud becomes progressively more separable: red trajectories tighten into a vertical bundle while blue ones swing farther outward, so class separation is something training creates, not something the architecture has at initialization.",
    "label": "Training iteration",
    "range": [
      0,
      3,
      1
    ],
    "default": 0
  },
  {
    "id": "layerStep",
    "type": "state",
    "teaches": "Each layer reshapes the same set of points: linear layers rotate/stretch the cloud, relu folds it against the axes, and softmax collapses it onto the class simplex — the separation is built up one transformation at a time.",
    "label": "Forward pass",
    "states": [
      "input",
      "linear 1",
      "relu 1",
      "linear 2",
      "relu 2",
      "linear 3",
      "softmax"
    ],
    "default": "input"
  },
  {
    "id": "traceDatapoint",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "A single datapoint keeps its identity through the whole network; tracing one red or one blue point upward shows the continuity between layerwise transformations and its final destination in the class bundle."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitStack",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The planes are 2D activation spaces stacked along a depth axis — rotating reveals that the trajectories are genuinely 3D curves and that point positions within each plane, not just their vertical order, carry the information."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Initialization: classes intermingled",
    "narration": "At iteration 0 the red and blue points stay mixed all the way up the stack; even the softmax plane shows the two colors overlapping.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 0,
      "layerStep": "softmax"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Start at the input plane",
    "narration": "The bottom plane holds the raw data: one dense central cluster with red and blue thoroughly intermingled.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 0,
      "layerStep": "input"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "First linear + relu",
    "narration": "The first linear layer stretches and rotates the cloud, then relu folds everything lying past the axes back toward them, producing the first hint of structure.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 2,
      "layerStep": "relu 1"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Deeper layers pull the classes apart",
    "narration": "By the second relu the red points have begun collecting into a narrow column while blue points bow outward to one side.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 2,
      "layerStep": "relu 2"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Late training, full forward pass",
    "narration": "At the final iteration the softmax plane shows a tight red bundle and blue points flung far to the opposite side — a clean, confident separation.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 3,
      "layerStep": "softmax"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render planes as THREE.LineSegments outlines plus a low-opacity grid; points as a single THREE.Points cloud per plane with per-vertex color, or InstancedMesh of small spheres for reliable hover picking. Trajectories: one LineSegments/Line2 per datapoint built from a CatmullRom through its 7 plane positions, with a dashed material (LineDashedMaterial, call computeLineDistances). Use additive-free normal blending with opacity ~0.3 so overlapping curves darken naturally. Precompute the 7-plane point positions for iteration keyframes t=0,1,2,3 and lerp between them so the slider scrub is smooth. layerStep drives a highlight: active plane outline turns black and thickens, incoming trajectory segments go to full opacity while all other segments drop to ~0.06, and planes above the active one fade to 0.2. Hover: raycast against the point cloud on any plane, resolve to a datapoint index, then set that datapoint's full 6-segment polyline to opacity 1.0 with a thicker dashed stroke and enlarge its dots on every plane; show a small tooltip with class and per-layer coordinates. Legend column and the 'Training iteration' arrow are HTML/CSS overlays anchored to the canvas so text stays crisp and monospaced (labels in a monospace font inside rounded rects with 1px dark border). OrbitControls limited to a modest azimuth/polar range around the isometric pose so the figure never loses its recognizable stacked-diamond reading.

